description:
I encountered the following exception:

```
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.AssertionError: unknown trans log instruction '4' at log offset: 2678772
        at org.mapdb.StoreWAL.verifyLogFile(StoreWAL.java:754)
        at org.mapdb.StoreWAL.<init>(StoreWAL.java:66)
        at org.mapdb.DBMaker.extendStoreWAL(DBMaker.java:923)
        at org.mapdb.DBMaker.makeEngine(DBMaker.java:718)
        at org.mapdb.DBMaker.makeTxMaker(DBMaker.java:686)
       <Application specific code>
```

The error occurred after loading a file based map store heavily under roughly 4 minutes from four threads and abruptly killing the Java process. Test was run on Windows 7, using Java 7 and a SSD disk. It seemed in this case that the WAL did not get a chance to completely flush it's entries to disk leaving it in an invalid state.

After removing the <code>.t</code> file the store opens fine. The corrupt <code>.t</code> is available and can be provided (it's about 2.5 MB in size)

As the WAL file always runs the risk of being corrupted on disk I think a more robust handling would be good, I see some alternatives (without knowing all the internal details):
- The WAL is parsed and only the valid entries are applied, incomplete entries are discarded
- A policy setting automatically removes the corrupted WAL file allowing the rest of the application to bootstrap

In both cases suitable warning messages should of course be logged.

The code that interacted with the HashMap is below (it's a Hazelcast <code>MapStore</code> implementation):

```
public class MapDbMapStoreV2 implements MapStore<Object, Object>, MapLoaderLifecycleSupport {
    private HTreeMap<Object, Object> dbReadOnlyMap;
    private DB readOnlyDb;
    private TxMaker txMaker;
    private String mapName;

    @Override
    public void init(HazelcastInstance hazelcastInstance, Properties properties, String mapName) {
        File dbFile = new File(properties.getProperty("dbFile"));
        dbFile = dbFile.getAbsoluteFile();
        this.mapName = mapName;
        //
        txMaker = DBMaker.newFileDB(dbFile).closeOnJvmShutdown().makeTxMaker();
        // NB! For DB initially does not exists the read-only must be opened after the TxMaker which will create the files on disk
        readOnlyDb = DBMaker.newFileDB(dbFile).transactionDisable().readOnly().closeOnJvmShutdown().make();
        dbReadOnlyMap = readOnlyDb.getHashMap(mapName);
    }

    private abstract class MapStoreTxBlock implements TxBlock {
        @Override
        public void tx(DB db) throws TxRollbackException {
            doMapTx(db.getHashMap(mapName));
        }

        public abstract void doMapTx(HTreeMap<Object, Object> txMap);
    }

    private void doTx(MapStoreTxBlock txBlock) {
        txMaker.execute(txBlock);
    }

    @Override
    public void destroy() {
        if (readOnlyDb != null && !readOnlyDb.isClosed()) {
            readOnlyDb.close();
            readOnlyDb = null;
        }
        if (txMaker != null) {
            txMaker.close();
            txMaker = null;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public Object load(Object key) {
        return dbReadOnlyMap.get(key);
    }

    @Override
    public Map<Object, Object> loadAll(Collection<Object> keys) {
        HashMap<Object, Object> valueMap = new HashMap<>(keys.size());
        for (Object key : keys) {
            valueMap.put(key, dbReadOnlyMap.get(key));
        }
        return valueMap;
    }

    @Override
    public Set<Object> loadAllKeys() {
        return dbReadOnlyMap.keySet();
    }

    @Override
    public void store(final Object key, final Object value) {
        doTx(new MapStoreTxBlock() {
            @Override
            public void doMapTx(HTreeMap<Object, Object> txMap) {
                txMap.put(key, value);
            }
        });
    }

    @Override
    public void storeAll(final Map<Object, Object> map) {
        doTx(new MapStoreTxBlock() {
            @Override
            public void doMapTx(HTreeMap<Object, Object> txMap) {
                txMap.putAll(map);
            }
        });
    }

    @Override
    public void delete(final Object key) {
        doTx(new MapStoreTxBlock() {
            @Override
            public void doMapTx(HTreeMap<Object, Object> txMap) {
                txMap.remove(key);
            }
        });
    }

    @Override
    public void deleteAll(final Collection<Object> keys) {
        doTx(new MapStoreTxBlock() {
            @Override
            public void doMapTx(HTreeMap<Object, Object> txMap) {
                for (Object key : keys) {
                    txMap.remove(key);
                }
            }
        });
    }
}
```

