protected void init() {
    if (!initialized.compareAndSet(false, true)) {

    }

    ....
}
```

description:
Netty Version: 4.0.24.Final

I use netty behind HAProxy and noticed some odd behavior: to close a connection HAProxy will send a tcp RST. Since this is standard behavior I made a handler which would suppress this exception. I inserted it towards the end of the pipeline, but it never got called. Instead I would see 

```
io.netty.channel.DefaultChannelPipeline$TailContext exceptionCaught
WARNING: An exceptionCaught() event was fired, and it reached at the tail of the pipeline. It usually means the last handler in the pipeline did not handle the exception.
java.io.IOException: Connection reset by peer
```

However, if the handler is at the front of the pipeline it will properly suppress the reset exception.

I created an example which reproduces the error sometimes. I think it's less frequent since it is a shorter pipeline. (Notice the two different `EventExecutorGroup`'s

Example Server:

``` java
package com.clover.netty;

import io.netty.bootstrap.ServerBootstrap;
import io.netty.buffer.PooledByteBufAllocator;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelHandlerContext;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelInitializer;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelOption;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelPipeline;
import io.netty.channel.nio.NioEventLoopGroup;
import io.netty.channel.socket.SocketChannel;
import io.netty.channel.socket.nio.NioServerSocketChannel;
import io.netty.util.concurrent.DefaultEventExecutorGroup;
import io.netty.util.concurrent.EventExecutorGroup;



public class NettyServer {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    ServerBootstrap serverBootstrap = new ServerBootstrap();
    final NioEventLoopGroup eventLoopGroup = new NioEventLoopGroup();
    serverBootstrap.group(eventLoopGroup)
        .channel(NioServerSocketChannel.class)
        .childOption(ChannelOption.ALLOCATOR, new PooledByteBufAllocator(false));

    final EventExecutorGroup eventExecutors1 = new DefaultEventExecutorGroup(1);
    final EventExecutorGroup eventExecutors2 = new DefaultEventExecutorGroup(1);

    serverBootstrap.childHandler(
        new ChannelInitializer<SocketChannel>() {
          @Override
          protected void initChannel(SocketChannel socketChannel) throws Exception {
            ChannelPipeline p = socketChannel.pipeline();
            p.addLast(eventExecutors1, new ExecutorGroupHandler("group 1") {
              @Override
              public void exceptionCaught(ChannelHandlerContext ctx, Throwable cause) throws Exception {
                super.exceptionCaught(ctx, cause);
                ctx.fireExceptionCaught(cause);
              }
            });

            p.addLast(eventExecutors2, new ExecutorGroupHandler("group 2"));
            }
        }
    );
    serverBootstrap.bind("0.0.0.0", 8080).sync();
  }

  public static class ExecutorGroupHandler extends ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter {
    private final String executorGroupName;
    public ExecutorGroupHandler(String executorGroupName) {
      this.executorGroupName = executorGroupName;
    }

    @Override
    public void exceptionCaught(ChannelHandlerContext ctx, Throwable cause) throws Exception {
      System.out.println("got error on " + executorGroupName + " " + cause);
    }

    @Override
    public void handlerRemoved(ChannelHandlerContext ctx) throws Exception {
      System.out.println("Handler removed from " + executorGroupName);
    }
  }
}
```

and a client used to send tcp RST:

``` ruby
require 'socket'

socket = TCPSocket.new 'localhost', '8080'

linger = [1,0].pack('ii')
socket.setsockopt(Socket::SOL_SOCKET, Socket::SO_LINGER, linger)

socket.close
```

When I make a request with the ruby client. Log messages are printed in one of two orders:

1)

```
got error on group 1 java.io.IOException: Connection reset by peer
got error on group 2 java.io.IOException: Connection reset by peer
Handler removed from group 2
Handler removed from group 1
```

2)

```
Handler removed from group 2
got error on group 1 java.io.IOException: Connection reset by peer
Handler removed from group 1
Nov 19, 2014 3:33:14 PM io.netty.channel.DefaultChannelPipeline$TailContext exceptionCaught
WARNING: An exceptionCaught() event was fired, and it reached at the tail of the pipeline. It usually means the last handler in the pipeline did not handle the exception.
java.io.IOException: Connection reset by peer
    at sun.nio.ch.FileDispatcherImpl.read0(Native Method)
    at sun.nio.ch.SocketDispatcher.read(SocketDispatcher.java:39)
    at sun.nio.ch.IOUtil.readIntoNativeBuffer(IOUtil.java:223)
    ...
```

It looks like in the second case the last handler gets removed before the exception reaches that point in the pipeline.

System Information:

```
java -version
java version "1.7.0_71"
Java(TM) SE Runtime Environment (build 1.7.0_71-b14)
Java HotSpot(TM) 64-Bit Server VM (build 24.71-b01, mixed mode)
```

OS: Mac 10.10.1 Yosemite

